  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Dell Federal Systems L P obligated $2.3B in FY2026 to date across 651 awards, with an average award size of $3.6M and obligations up 75.3% year over year. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer, obligating $1.1B, followed by the Department of Veterans Affairs at $553M and the Department of Homeland Security at $300M. Software-oriented markets dominate FY2026 to date, led by Software Publishers at $2B and a second Software Publishers entry at $126M, with Electronic Computer Manufacturing at $120M and Other Computer Related Services at $70.1M. Fiscal-year obligations show a recent rise: FY21 was $1.6B, FY22 was $1.7B, FY23 was $1.4B, FY24 was $1.5B, FY25 was $1.3B, and FY26 is $2.3B. The top ten buying agencies mix defense, health, and civilian customers, including Department of Health and Human Services at $140M and Department of Commerce at $62.4M. Dell Federal Systems L P’s federal work in FY2026 to date is concentrated in large software and computing contracts with a strong single-agency exposure to the Department of Defense.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Which agencies drive Dell Federal Systems L P’s federal revenue in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "The Department of Defense obligated $1.1B, making it the single largest buyer. The Department of Veterans Affairs obligated $553M and the Department of Homeland Security obligated $300M. Civilian agencies with meaningful obligations include the Department of Health and Human Services at $140M and the Department of Commerce at $62.4M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What markets and contract sizes characterize Dell Federal Systems L P’s FY2026 work?",
        "body": "Software markets are the largest by dollar value, with Software Publishers totaling $2B and an additional Software Publishers entry at $126M. Electronic Computer Manufacturing accounted for $120M and Other Computer Related Services accounted for $70.1M. The average award size is $3.6M across 651 awards, so work is a mix of large and mid-size contracts."
      }
    ]
